  <h1>Personal AI Agents Compared: OpenAI Dots vs Meta Muse vs Grok Bot vs Instinct</h1>
  <p class="meta">By Tycoon Clap · October 1, 2026 · 7 min read</p>
</header>

<div class="hook">
  <p class="big">4 personal AI agents launched in 7 weeks.</p>
  <p class="punch">OpenAI Dots. Meta Muse. Grok Bot. Instinct.</p>
</div>

<article>
<p>Everyone's calling it the year of AI agents. But most people still use AI like a search box. Ask. Read. Copy. Paste.</p>
<p><strong>Personal agents flip that.</strong> They get their own computer in the cloud. They sign into your apps. They keep working after you close the tab.</p>
<p>This is a side-by-side look at all four: what each does, what it costs, and where it can hurt you.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-an-ai-agent">What is an AI agent? (Agentic AI definition)</h2>
<div class="answer"><strong>An AI agent is an AI system that pursues a goal on its own. It plans steps, uses tools and apps, and takes actions for you, instead of only replying to prompts.</strong></div>
<p>That is the working agentic AI definition. "Agentic AI" is the broader label for systems that act with autonomy rather than wait for the next instruction.</p>
<p>An AI chat bot answers. An agent does the job.</p>

<h2 id="chatbot-vs-agent">AI chat bot vs. AI agent: what changed</h2>
<p>A classic AI chat bot lives in a tab. You type, it replies, and it forgets the job when you leave. A personal agent has three things a chatbot doesn't:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Its own workspace.</strong> A cloud computer and browser it can operate.</li>
  <li><strong>Your logins.</strong> Access to email, calendar, Slack, payments and more.</li>
  <li><strong>Persistence.</strong> It keeps working while you're offline.</li>
</ul>
<p>That combination is why <strong>AI automation</strong> suddenly feels different. You're not automating a step. You're delegating a responsibility.</p>
</article>

