<article>
<h2 id="openai-dots">OpenAI Dots</h2>
<p>OpenAI announced Dots at DevDay on September 29. Each one is powered by GPT-6 Astra and positioned as an always-on agent. People also search for it as "Open AI Dots," but the name is just Dots.</p>
<p>Each dot works on its own cloud computer that you can open to inspect its work, and it can reach more than 4,000 apps through OpenAI's plugin ecosystem. Linking your personal computer is optional and starts switched off. You set rules for what a dot can do automatically and what needs your approval.</p>
<p>Your first dot comes with a Pro or Business Premium plan at no extra cost. OpenAI is also working with Microsoft to connect Dots to Agent 365 security controls.</p>
<p class="verdict"><strong>Verdict:</strong> the strongest fit if you already live in ChatGPT, especially for developers and work tasks.</p>

<h2 id="meta-muse">Meta Muse</h2>
<p>Meta launched Muse for adults 18 and over, aimed at schedules, shopping and turning long-term goals into action plans. For now it's only in the U.S. You can reach it through a dedicated app or through WhatsApp.</p>
<p>It runs on a dedicated virtual machine in Meta's cloud, with a built-in browser you can watch. Meta says it can send emails, book travel, fill out forms and make purchases through Stripe's Link. There's a free tier plus $20 and $100 monthly plans.</p>
<p>On privacy, Meta's AI chief says the agent never sees your actual passwords or payment details. But by default, queries to Meta's models can be used by the company, with an option to turn that off.</p>
<p class="verdict"><strong>Verdict:</strong> the easiest on-ramp for everyday life admin. The open question is whether you trust Meta with the data.</p>

<h2 id="grok-bot">Grok Bot</h2>
<p>xAI launched Grok Bot in beta on August 11, 2026. Each bot gets its own cloud computer and signs into the apps you already use. It's a separate product from the Grok chatbot you know from X.</p>
<p>What sets it apart is the team model. You can put several bots in a group chat, and they can message each other and pass work along. When there's no plugin, it falls back to a browser, and you step in to type credentials when a login stops it.</p>
<p>Since August 26, it's included with SuperGrok plans and several Cursor plans. Pricing has shifted since launch, so check xAI's page before you buy.</p>
<p class="verdict"><strong>Verdict:</strong> best for builders and small teams who want specialist agents working together. It is still labeled beta.</p>

<h2 id="instinct">Instinct</h2>
<p>Instinct is the odd one out. It's an invite-only assistant you text or call, with no dashboard to learn. It does the work by controlling a persistent cloud desktop with full browser and app access.</p>
<p>It is also the hype and risk story. Instinct announced a $1 billion Series C, and a feature called the Trusted Person Network lets one person's agent coordinate with another's. Invites have been resold on eBay.</p>
<p>The catch is access. It asks for email, messages, screen, audio and location at once, and its terms authorize it to enter binding agreements on your behalf.</p>
<p class="verdict"><strong>Verdict:</strong> the most "it just does it" experience, with the most trust required.</p>

<h2 id="which-to-pick">Side by side: which should you pick?</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Already pay for ChatGPT Pro?</strong> Start with Dots.</li>
  <li><strong>Want life admin on WhatsApp, and you're in the U.S.?</strong> Try Muse.</li>
  <li><strong>Want a team of specialist agents?</strong> Look at Grok Bot.</li>
  <li><strong>Want to text a problem and forget it?</strong> Join the Instinct waitlist, and read the terms first.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="risks">The part nobody puts in the launch video</h2>
<p>These are AI systems with keys to your life. Four things to check before you connect anything:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Permissions.</strong> What can it do without asking? Set approval rules first.</li>
  <li><strong>Data use.</strong> Is your data used for model training by default?</li>
  <li><strong>Money.</strong> Can it spend, and is there a cap?</li>
  <li><strong>Mistakes.</strong> OpenAI itself says dots can still make mistakes. Start with low-stakes work.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="business">What this means for businesses</h2>
<p>Employees will bring these agents to work whether IT approves them or not. Grok Bot, for one, reaches people through consumer subscriptions rather than a security review.</p>
<p>The agent is the easy part. What decides whether AI automation pays off is clean data, defined workflows and clear permissions. At Tycoon Clap we build the data pipelines and automation layers these agents depend on. An agent plugged into messy systems just makes mistakes faster.</p>
